"Leave a bicycle outside through a few weeks of rain, and something happens to the chain. Orange-brown spots appear, and the chain feels rough. Leave a pair of wet scissors in a drawer and the same thing happens. We call it rust. But what is rust, and where does it come from?": "227b6686ea1c",
"Leave a bicycle outside through a few weeks of rain, and something happens to the chain. Orange-brown spots appear, and the chain feels rough. Leave a pair of wet scissors in a drawer and the same thing happens. We call it rust. But what is rust, and where does it come from? Rust is not dirt that lands on the metal. It is the metal itself, changed into something new. Iron joins with oxygen from the air, and water helps the change happen. The new stuff is called iron oxide. It is reddish brown, crumbly, and weak. So rust needs three things: iron, oxygen, and water. Take away any one of the three and the rusting almost stops. A dry nail in a dry room stays shiny for a very long time. A nail covered in oil stays shiny too, because the oil keeps water and air away from it. Salt does the opposite: it makes iron rust much faster. That is why bikes, scooters, and railings near the sea rust so quickly. Rust is especially bad news for iron because it is loose and flaky. It even takes up more room than the iron it came from, so it cracks and falls off. Then fresh iron underneath is open to the air and water, and it rusts too. Given enough time, a whole piece of iron can turn to rust. Aluminum is luckier: it forms a thin, tight skin that protects the metal below. So people protect iron by keeping one of the three things away. Paint and oil are walls against water and air. The Eiffel Tower gets about 60 tons of new paint every seven years. Another way is to coat iron with zinc, which keeps protecting it even when it is scratched. And stainless steel has chromium mixed in, which makes an invisible film that shields it. You can fight rust too. Dry your bike after the rain, and put a drop of oil on the chain. Dry knives, scissors, and iron pans before you put them away. If the paint on something is chipped, tell an adult so it can be touched up. A little care now saves a lot of metal later.": "507c0372b644",
